{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall tell your son, etc.</b> One must know why it did not say ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״, for we find the word haggadah employed by our Rabbis of blessed memory who said (Shabbat 87a) that one should intend in it things as hard as sinews. Further, it is difficult that it said <b>on that day</b>, and immediately nearby it concludes ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Mekhilta Bo, chapter 17, and in the Haggadah) [to mean] at the time of matzah and maror, etc., which is at night and not by day; if so, it should have said only ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ לֵאמֹר בַּעֲבוּר זֶה״ — and I already know the time of the haggadah, that it is at night. Further, one must understand its saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, when it already said ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ״." "<b>And we shall see</b> that it intends according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Pesachim 116a-b) that one begins with disgrace and concludes with praise. That is, one speaks the disgrace of the fathers' ancestors, for there was nothing praiseworthy — and this is its saying <b>וְהִגַּדְתָּ</b>: things as hard as sinews, from which a person's heart is stubbornly knotted; and afterward one concludes with praise, and this is its saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>: praiseworthy sayings that gladden the heart and sustain it. And by saying <b>on that day</b>, He informed with the sweetness of the language of the righteous that that night is called a day, not a night — as it says (Psalms 139:12) ״וְלַיְלָה כַּיּוֹם יָאִיר״ — and He was not concerned lest one err to say it means actual daytime, despite the fact that it concludes ״בַּעֲבוּר זֶה״ which is at the time of matzah, etc., as our Rabbis of blessed memory said. Or perhaps it juxtaposed ״on that day״ with ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ״ because this miracle too is included within the mitzvah of haggadah." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend by saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b>: since it said ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ״, one might think that if he has a son [he must tell]; if he has no son, is he exempt? Scripture therefore says ״לֵאמֹר״ — that in any case he must tell even to himself. And if you say: since in any case he must tell even to himself, if so why did it say ״לְבִנְךָ״? Or perhaps it is through the extra word that I understand it so, and not without the extra word. And it is further possible that it alludes by saying <b>וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ</b> that if he tells the haggadah as said in the matter, HaShem will grant him merit to tell it to his son; and lest you err to say exclusively [to one's son], therefore it said ״לֵאמֹר״." "<b>Because of this.</b> Or perhaps it alluded in the word ״זֶה״ the twelve commandments inscribed in the festival of Pesach: three things — pesach, matzah, and maror — and the haggadah, and the seven days of the festival, and the sanctification of the first day and the second — behold, twelve, equal to the numerical value of ״זֶה״, and this is its saying <b>because of this was done</b>, etc. — even though the seven days of the festival, in terms of the 613 commandments, are not counted as seven separate commandments."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:38.720752544Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}